 say we can understand them  because people who speak well usually  have done a lot of listening and have  done a lot of speaking because when we  engage with people in the language we  also pick up on their accent alright we  normally we're very easily influenced by  what we hear around us so if you do the  normal things in terms of listening and  reading and speaking your pronunciation  will improve however there are some  specific things I think that can be done  to improve one's pronunciation to get  closer to the desired accent which as I  say it could be French from France from  southern France  it could be German from Bavaria it could  be Japanese from the Osaka region  whatever it is that we want to imitate  we're gonna listen to it and over and  over again in order to get that  pronunciation in us so but some bits of  advice first of all we should not be too  influenced by our own writing system so  if we are used to reading in English we  mustn't assume that Spanish  pronunciation gives the same value to  the writing to the letters as we do in  English I can remember being in Japan  and I introduced Canadian English  speaker to a mr. Kotter  and they said mr. Kittel I mean I just  finished saying cut oh and they say  caked-on ignore the value of the letters  in your own language the reverse is also  true Japanese people who learn English  and who use katakana use their own or  hiragana their own  usually katakana their own writing  system as a guide to the pronunciation  in English they will be in trouble you  have to rely on what you hear you have  to see the relationship between the  language as written in the target  language and the way it's pronounced you  have to focus on them and hear it and if  you do that enough the brain will get  used to it so you can't pronounce  correctly what you don't hear so you  first of all you have to get used to  allowing yourself to hear how the  language is pronounced without any  preconceived ideas coming from your own  language okay second of all so to review  here you have to do a lot of reading and  a lot of listening let the sounds bounce  on your brain you have to be open to the  way the language is pronounced not have  preconceived ideas about how you think  it should be pronounced based on your  own writing system and third of all you  have to want to there has to be an  emotional attachment to that  pronunciation otherwise there's a  tendency to hang back in what's familiar  what's comfortable but you have to be  willing to say okay I am Chinese  I am Russia I am French and you pretend  to be that nationality you pretend to be  a native speaker of that language even  though you don't achieve it you pretend  to be it there has to be that commitment  to pronouncing okay  all the while accepting the fact that  you needn't be perfect you needn't be  accent free you want to speak well in  the language you want to pretend you are  one of them but you won't you'll fall  short okay you'll parcel so that has to  be a part of the equation you can like  through a lot of listing you will notice  how the language is pronounced you'll  notice most things but there are some  things you won't notice so there I think  it's a good idea to read sort of a  description of pronunciation in this  language you can read it when you start  out it won't make much of an impression  because you don't yet have a sense of  the language it might help a bit I  wouldn't try to nail anything down and  then at various times you can re-read  sort of an explanation of the  pronunciation and you may pick up a few  things that will be helpful as you try  to sort of notice and imitate the  language to me that's the main thing be  relaxed about it  don't be hung up about it the more  relaxed you are the better the more you  engage with the language the better the  better you use the language the better  your pronunciation will be and the more  you speak the better you'll get and you  have to be open you have to be open one  thing I should point out that's probably  beyond the reach of many people but it's  something that I'm convinced of and that  is that when we only have one language  say English or Japanese or French then  we are very much influenced by that  language if we learn a second language  we are now exposed to more sounds and so  therefore when we go to Laura learn a  third or fourth language we are more  open more flexible when it comes to  sounds so obviously if you're learning  your first second language then you are  dealing with that problem so you have to  be as open as possible to the new sounds  if you choose to learn a third language  you'll probably find that pronunciation  in that third language will be easier so  those are my bits of advice on  pronunciation keep an open mind  continue your main input activities  speak as much as you can don't worry  about it but allow yourself  pretend like imitate be the person of  that language that you are learning and  good luck to you in your translation bye  for now
 

